<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=edwin-smith-papyrus">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edwin Smith Papyrus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1600 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century-bce">17th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Edwin Smith <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> is an ancient Egyptian medical text and the oldest known surgical treatise on trauma, copied c. 1600 BCE, probably at Thebes, from an original that may date from the Old Kingdom. Written in hieratic on a scroll 4.68 metres long, it sets out 48 cases of injury, each with examination, diagnosis, prognosis and treatment, and largely avoids magic. Named after the American Egyptologist who bought it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a> in 1862, it has been kept at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Academy of Medicine since 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edwin-smith-papyrus.jpg" alt="Edwin Smith Papyrus" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Dahl · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edwin_Smith_Papyrus_v2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 1600 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Thebes, Egypt</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The surviving copy may reproduce an older original of the Old Kingdom; the suggestion that Imhotep wrote it is conjecture.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus<span>c. 3000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=edwin-smith-papyrus">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
